esson 19: Type Guards in TypeScript 🚀
Type Guard ka use hota hai check karne ke liye ki kisi variable ka actual type kya hai, taaki TypeScript safely uske saath kaam kar sake.
1. Problem samjho
Maan lo variable mein string ya number, dono aa sakte hain.
function printValue(value: string | number) {
  console.log(value);
}


Yahan value ka type string | number hai, jise Union Type kehte hain.
Agar humein sirf string hone par uppercase karna ho, toh kya karenge?
2. typeof se Type Guard
function printValue(value: string | number) {
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    console.log(value.toUpperCase());
  } else {
    console.log(value * 2);
  }
}

printValue("hello"); // HELLO
printValue(10);      // 20


typeof value === "string"

TypeScript samajh jaata hai ki if block ke andar value string hai.

else block

Yahan TypeScript samajhta hai ki value number hai.

3. Array.isArray() se Type Guard
function showData(data: string | string[]) {
  if (Array.isArray(data)) {
    console.log(data.length);
  } else {
    console.log(data.toUpperCase());
  }
}

showData(["apple", "mango"]); // 2
showData("hello");            // HELLO


- Array.isArray(data) → check karta hai ki value array hai ya nahi.
- Array hone par .length se elements ki count milti hai.
- String hone par .toUpperCase() use kar sakte hain.
4. in operator se Type Guard
Jab do object types ki properties alag hon, tab in operator useful hai.
type Car = {
  drive: () => void;
};

type Bike = {
  ride: () => void;
};

function useVehicle(vehicle: Car | Bike) {
  if ("drive" in vehicle) {
    vehicle.drive();
  } else {
    vehicle.ride();
  }
}


Yahan "drive" in vehicle check karta hai ki object mein drive property hai ya nahi.
